WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:03.530
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.530 --> 00:00:10.089
Have a look at these beautiful ferrofluid
simulations from a previous paper.

00:00:10.089 --> 00:00:16.180
These are fluids that have magnetic properties
and thus respond to an external magnetic field,

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:21.800
and you are seeing correctly, they are able
to even climb things.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:27.360
And the best part is that simulator was so
accurate that we could run it side-by-side

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:31.820
with real-life footage, and see that they
run very similarly.

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:32.840
Excellent.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:37.500
Now, running these simulations took a considerable
amount of time.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:43.680
To address this, a followup paper appeared
that showcased a surface-only formulation.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:44.800
What does that mean?

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:50.100
Well, a key observation here was that for
a class of ferrofluids, we don’t have to

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:56.059
compute how the magnetic forces act on the
entirety of the 3D fluid domain, we only have

00:00:56.059 --> 00:00:59.490
to compute them on the surface of the model.

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:01.579
So what does this get us?

00:01:01.579 --> 00:01:07.630
Well, these amazing fluid labyrinths, and
all of these ferrofluid simulations, but…

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:08.630
faster!

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:15.630
So, remember, the first work did something
new, but took a very long time, and the second

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:19.530
work improved it to make it faster and more
practical.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:22.810
Please remember this for later in this video.

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:29.119
And now let’s fast forward to today’s
paper, this new work can also simulate ferrofluids,

00:01:29.119 --> 00:01:35.240
and not only that, but also supports a broader
range of magnetic phenomena, including rigid

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:39.650
and deformable magnetic bodies and two-way
coupling too!

00:01:39.650 --> 00:01:41.380
Oh my!

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:46.070
That is sensational, but first, what do these
terms mean exactly?

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:51.710
Let’s perform four experiments, and after
you watch them, I promise that you will understand

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:52.869
all about them.

00:01:52.869 --> 00:01:57.320
Let’s look at the rigid bodies first in
experiment number one.

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:00.140
Iron box versus magnet.

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:06.850
We are starting out slow, and now, we are
waiting for the attraction to kick in, and

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:09.270
there we go.

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.100
Wonderful.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:14.700
Experiment number two, deformable magnetic
bodies.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:19.030
In other words, magnetic lotus versus a moving
magnet.

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:24.620
This one is absolutely beautiful, look at
how the the petals here are modeled as thin

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:29.860
elastic sheets that dance around in the presence
of a moving magnet.

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:35.080
And if you think this is dancing, stay tuned,
there will be an example with even better

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:37.370
dance moves in a moment.

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:40.580
And experiment number three, two-way coupling.

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:44.810
We noted this coupling thing earlier, so,
what does that mean?

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:49.709
What coupling means is that here, the water
can have an effect on the magnet, and the

00:02:49.709 --> 00:02:56.190
two-way part means that in return, the magnet
can also have an effect on the water as well.

00:02:56.190 --> 00:03:00.940
This is excellent, because we don’t have
to think about the limitations of the simulation,

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:07.599
we can just drop nearly anything into our
simulation domain, be it a fluid, solid, magnetic

00:03:07.599 --> 00:03:12.720
or not, and we can expect that their interactions
are going to be modeled properly.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:13.770
Outstanding.

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:20.629
And, I promised some more dancing, so here
goes, experiment number four, the dancing

00:03:20.629 --> 00:03:22.080
ferrofluid.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:28.550
I love how informative the compass is here,
it is a simple object that tells us how an

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:31.870
external magnetic field evolves over time.

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:34.550
I love this elegant solution.

00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:39.800
Normally, we have to visualize the magnetic
induction lines so we can better see why the

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:47.310
tentacles of a magnetic octopus move, or why
two ferrofluid droplets repel or attract each

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:48.610
other.

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:54.090
In this case, the authors opted for a much
more concise and elegant solution, and I also

00:03:54.090 --> 00:04:00.940
liked that the compass is not just a 2D overlay,
but a properly shaded 3D object with specular

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:03.280
reflections as well.

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:04.920
Excellent attention to detail.

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:07.670
This is really my kind of paper.

00:04:07.670 --> 00:04:14.280
Now, these simulations were not run on any
kind of supercomputer or a network of computers,

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:18.319
this runs on the processor of your consumer
machine at home.

00:04:18.319 --> 00:04:23.889
However, simulating even the simpler scenes
takes hours.

00:04:23.889 --> 00:04:27.050
For more complex scenes, even days.

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:31.990
And that’s not all, the ferrofluid with
the Yin-Yang symbol took nearly a month to

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:33.030
compute.

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:35.650
So, is that a problem?

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:38.279
No, no, of course not.

00:04:38.279 --> 00:04:39.889
Not in the slightest.

00:04:39.889 --> 00:04:46.740
Thanks to this paper, general magnetic simulations
that were previous impossible are now possible,

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:49.430
and don’t forget, research is a process.

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:53.810
As you saw in the example at the start of
this video with the surface-only ferrofluid

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:59.039
formulation, it may become much faster just
one more paper down the line.

00:04:59.039 --> 00:05:04.009
I wanted to show you the first two papers
in this video to demonstrate how quickly that

00:05:04.009 --> 00:05:05.719
can happen.

00:05:05.719 --> 00:05:11.020
And two more papers down the line, oh my,
then, the sky is the limit.

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:57.060
What a time to 
be alive!

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:06.549
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

